          "collection_date": "2024-05-23",
          "description": {
            "comment": "The Mount Graham red squirrel (Tamiasciurus hudsonicus grahamensis) is one of 25 subspecies of red squirrels found throughout North America. The Mount Graham subspecies, which is found only on the Pinaleno (Graham) Mountains of southeastern Arizona, was thought to have been extinct in the 1950's, but small numbers of squirrels were \"rediscovered\" in the 1970's. The squirrel was added to the federal endangered species list in 1987 by the U.S. Fish and Wildlife Service, when the estimated population in 1986 was fewer than 400 (Source: https://mgio.arizona.edu/mount-graham-red-squirrel). This cell line sample comes from a male individual in Mount Graham, Arizona procured by Bret Pasch for the Revive & Restore Biobanking Initiative, in collaboration with the US Fish and Wildlife Service and San Diego Zoo Wildlife Alliance. The sample was used to generate a high-quality reference genome for the Vertebrate Genomes Project (VGP). Sequencing and genome assembly were conducted at the Vertebrate Genome Lab (VGL) at the Rockefeller University, led by Erich D. Jarvis, Giulio Formenti, and Jennifer Balacco.",
